  1. CurveConvex2Token._checkReentrancyContext() makes a plain external CALL
  (CurveConvexLib(payable(LP_LIB)).checkReentrancyContext()), not a delegatecall, so inside the lib address(this) ==
  LP_LIB and the Curve pool sees msg.sender == LP_LIB. That part of the claim is accurate, and the receive() external
  payable comment ("it only will be done to this contract during the checkReentrancyContext function") confirms the lib
  really is the caller in this path.
  2. The claimed failure step is entirely in code that is not provided: it depends on the exact Curve V2
  (CurveCryptoSwap*) implementation burning _amount (=1) rather than the internally decremented amount (=_amount - 1
  = 0) from msg.sender. The in-repo comment ("Curve V2 does a -1 on the liquidity amount so set the amount removed to 1
  to avoid an underflow") shows the authors reasoned about exactly this arithmetic, and no Curve pool source is in the
  input to verify the burn target/amount. I cannot point at a line in the reviewed code that proves the revert; the
  claim rests on an assumed external-contract semantic.
  3. Even granting the assumption, the "permanently unliquidatable position" conclusion does not follow. The only state
  the check consumes is 1 wei of CURVE_POOL_TOKEN held by LP_LIB, and that balance is toppable up permissionlessly by
  anyone via an ordinary ERC20 transfer to LP_LIB — no access control, no vault accounting that rejects donated LP dust
  (the lib's LP balance is never validated anywhere in this file;
  _stakeLpTokens/_unstakeLpTokens/transferYieldTokenToOwner all operate on explicit amounts). A liquidator can therefore
  unblock _checkReentrancyContext for a dust cost and proceed, and repeated checks each consume only 1 wei. So there is
  no permanent, unrecoverable brick of the liquidation path; at worst this is a dust-precondition/griefing nuisance,
  below the claimed High and below Medium once the trivial permissionless remedy is accounted for.
  4. Additionally, CurveInterface.V2 is a constructor-supplied deployment parameter chosen by the privileged deployer; a
  configuration in which the reentrancy probe cannot execute would surface at deployment/integration time rather than
  being an unprivileged attacker's lever — the "attacker" here does nothing but hold a position.

